  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-6t-4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(5)=30.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30 & =(5)^{3}-3(5)^{2}-4(5)+C \\\\ 30 & =125-75-20+C \\\\ 30 & =30+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-4t & =0 \\\\ t({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =0 \\\\ t(t-4)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $t=-1,0,4.$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we discard solutions $t=-1,0.$ \nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$",
